                        "entry_id": "0cb5eedd-5e8b-38b4-9410-9e44ea8d69d1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The use of macaronic language; The mixing of two or more languages in a single work."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2eb45239-2224-4774-b259-4b1bc5991602",
                                "sense_id": "4dc752d9-eee2-3d7e-8a07-2cf3c1514c04",
                                "text": "Before quitting this division of macaronicism, we must not forget the amusing specimen given by Molière in the troisième intermede of Le Malade Imaginaire , where Argan the invalid is to be admitted a doctor ."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4811a27c-b5f1-42cc-a3e9-d3ec78dbddbc",
                                "sense_id": "4dc752d9-eee2-3d7e-8a07-2cf3c1514c04",
                                "text": "Klymasz argues that macaronicism is a comic device in The Street Where I Live, but he stops short of discussing the ways Haas uses macaronic humour to undermine the dominance of Anglo-Canadian culture."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "40a766df-d3a4-4c4c-9379-1d5b31b12087",
                                "sense_id": "4dc752d9-eee2-3d7e-8a07-2cf3c1514c04",
                                "text": "Readers of seventeenth-century Polish texts— from baroque poetry through polemical pamphlets to forensic prose—are used to deciphering Polish-Latin macaronicisms."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8bbdcee5-4c78-4fd0-8c29-8dd0bb871a08",
                                "sense_id": "4dc752d9-eee2-3d7e-8a07-2cf3c1514c04",
                                "text": "The playfulness of macaronicism may have been another contributory factor: Omnegatherum, that is to say, may be a self-deprecating acknowledgement of the impossibility of the encyclopaedic dream and the inability of any compiler or miscellanist to contain a total body of knowledge within the bounds of a single book."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
